# Reusable, NON-EXPIRING, UNTAGGED auth key.
# Untagged => user-owned node => the autogroup:self ACL rule matches.
# After first boot, DISABLE KEY EXPIRY in the admin console or you WILL be locked out.
TS_AUTHKEY=tskey-auth-REPLACE-ME

# GitHub PAT — logs in gh and git-over-HTTPS with no interactive flow.
# Mint a CLASSIC token at https://github.com/settings/tokens/new and tick repo,
# read:org, workflow, and admin:ssh_signing_key — that last one is what lets the
# signing key below register itself. Fine-grained equivalent: contents,
# pull requests, and SSH signing keys (read+write).
# NOT the output of `gh auth token`: that is a gho_ OAuth token belonging to the
# GitHub CLI's app, absent from the tokens page, impossible to re-scope, and
# revocable only by revoking the CLI everywhere you use it.
# It is written to ~/.devaloy_secrets (0600) AND stored as a real gh login in
# ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml, so treat the home volume as holding a live credential.
# The stored login is what non-login-shell callers read — the Orca app's GitHub
# pickers among them, since only .zshenv and .bashrc export the variable itself.
# Leave empty to skip and use gh auth login by hand — gh refuses that flow while
# this variable is set in your shell.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Claude Code OAuth token — logs the agent in with no `/login` on the box.
# Generate it on your own machine with `claude setup-token` (needs Pro/Max or
# a Team/Enterprise seat) and paste the printed token here. Valid for a year,
# never self-refreshes. Written to ~/.devaloy_secrets (0600) like the PAT above.
# Leave empty to run `/login` inside the container instead — while this is set,
# an interactive login on the box is ignored.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Repos to clone into ~/projects on every boot. Whitespace-separated, in any of
# three forms: owner/repo shorthand, a full https:// URL, or the git@host:path
# SSH form. Each one lands in ~/projects/<repo>, and a repo already there is
# left alone, so adding a line and redeploying clones only the new one.
#
# Cloned over HTTPS through the gh credential helper, so a private repo needs
# GITHUB_TOKEN above. A single bad entry logs a warning and is skipped; it never
# stops the box from starting.
#
# Two repos of the same name from different owners collide on the directory
# name. The second one is skipped with a warning — clone it by hand under
# another name.
# DEVALOY_REPOS=mimukit/devaloy https://github.com/cli/cli git@github.com:you/private.git

# Who commits from this box. Leave both empty and the entrypoint asks GitHub
# who GITHUB_TOKEN belongs to, using that account's ID+login noreply address —
# always a verified address on the account, which signature verification needs.
# With neither these nor a token, every commit fails on "Author identity
# unknown".
# GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Your Name
# GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=1234567+you@users.noreply.github.com

# SSH key that signs commits, so GitHub marks them Verified instead of
# Unverified. Pass the key you ALREADY use — no need to generate one:
#
#   base64 -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 | tr -d '\n'
#
# Base64 on ONE line because a .env value cannot hold the newlines a key file
# needs, and it must have NO passphrase — nothing in the container can answer a
# prompt, so an encrypted key is rejected at boot. If yours has one, copy it and
# strip the passphrase from the copy: ssh-keygen -p -N '' -f <copy>.
#
# The PRIVATE key — not id_ed25519.pub, not a line from allowed_signers. A right
# value is several hundred characters and decodes to a BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY
# block; anything around 100 characters is the public half and is rejected. The
# entrypoint says which it got, on the container's stdout: docker compose logs
# devaloy, since that never reaches an SSH session on the box.
#
# The entrypoint registers the public half on your GitHub account by itself, as
# long as GITHUB_TOKEN above carries the admin:ssh_signing_key scope
# (fine-grained: "SSH signing keys" read+write). Without that scope it logs a
# link and you add it once by hand. GitHub checks signatures against keys listed
# under SSH *signing* keys — a key already on your account for authentication
# does NOT count, so it has to be listed under both types.
#
# Written to ~/.ssh/devaloy_signing (0600) in the home volume; treat that volume
# as holding a live credential. Leave empty to commit unsigned.
# GIT_SIGNING_SSH_KEY=

# Push notifications to your phone via ntfy (https://ntfy.sh) when an agent on
# this box blocks for permission or finishes a long turn. OFF unless NTFY_TOPIC
# is set. The topic string IS the credential: an ntfy.sh topic is unreserved, so
# anyone who knows it can subscribe and read every message. Pick a LONG, RANDOM
# string (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 16`), not a guessable name. Messages carry
# metadata only — agent, repo basename, state, elapsed, host — never prompt or
# assistant text. Subscribe by opening https://ntfy.sh/<your-topic> in the app.
# NTFY_TOPIC=devaloy-CHANGE-ME-to-something-long-and-random
# NTFY_SERVER=https://ntfy.sh
# NTFY_TOKEN=

# Build the optional Orca runtime into the image, so the Orca desktop and
# mobile apps can pair with this box. Off by default — it takes the image from
# 683 MB to 1.6 GB. This is a BUILD arg: changing it needs `docker compose up -d
# --build`, not a plain restart. There is no runtime kill switch.
# WITH_ORCA=false

# Build Docker Engine and Compose into the image, so a project's own
# docker-compose.yml can start its development stack on this box. Off by
# default. Like WITH_ORCA above this is a BUILD arg: changing it needs
# `docker compose up -d --build`, not a plain restart.
#
# The daemon runs INSIDE this container, and that is the point. Bind mounts
# resolve against /home/dev, and a published port lands in this container's
# network namespace — so `curl localhost:3000` works in an SSH session and
# `http://devaloy:3000` works from your phone. The host's Docker socket would
# give you neither, and would give any agent here root on the whole VPS.
#
# This key alone is not enough. A nested daemon needs authority over its own
# namespaces, and exactly one of the two keys below grants it. Which one you
# pick is a property of the HOST, not of this box. Set one, never both.
# WITH_DOCKER=false

# --- shape A: a shared host (a Dokploy VPS with other people's sites on it) ---
#
# Sysbox replaces runc on the host, maps this container's root onto an
# unprivileged host user, and runs the nested daemon with NO privilege at all.
# This is the shape to use anywhere the host has neighbours.
#
# It needs work on the host first, and that work includes one Docker restart
# that bounces every container on the machine. Read the wiki page before you
# start, and run `sudo scripts/host-resource-guard.sh --sysbox --check` to see
# what the host has now. Then:
#
#   WITH_DOCKER=true
#   DEVALOY_RUNTIME=sysbox-runc
#
# --- shape B: a host you own alone and would treat as disposable ---
#
# `privileged: true` is the simple answer and the dangerous one. It is
# all-or-nothing — there is no partial privileged — and it hands this container
# every host device. An agent in here can mount the host's disk and read and
# write every file on the machine. Never set this on a host with neighbours.
#
#   WITH_DOCKER=true
#   DEVALOY_PRIVILEGED=true
#
# Both default off, which is plain runc with no privilege — exactly what this
# box has always been.
# DEVALOY_RUNTIME=runc
# DEVALOY_PRIVILEGED=false

# Run the Paseo daemon, so the Paseo phone, desktop, browser and CLI clients can
# drive agents on this box. Off by default.
#
# Unlike WITH_ORCA above this is a RUNTIME variable, not a build arg — Paseo is
# an npm package that lands in the home volume, so `docker compose up -d` picks
# up a change with no --build and nothing enters the image. Setting it back to
# false stops the daemon on the next boot; it does not uninstall the CLI or
# rewrite ~/.paseo/config.json.
#
# The daemon binds the tailnet address only, on port 6767, with the relay
# disabled. Connect the app under Settings > Add host > Direct connection with
# SSL off, and keep Tailscale connected on the phone.
#
# The settings are not passed on the command line. Each boot merges the repo's
# config/paseo/config.json and the tailnet address into ~/.paseo/config.json,
# which is also the file every `paseo` on the box reads to find the daemon.
# Change a default there rather than on the box; keys the repo ships are
# rewritten on the next boot, and keys it does not ship survive.
# WITH_PASEO=false

# Serve Paseo's bundled web client from the daemon, so a browser on the tailnet
# is a full client with no app install. A separate key because the static UI
# loads without authentication.
#
# PASEO_PASSWORD is optional but strongly wanted with the web UI on: leave it
# empty and anything that reaches the tailnet address gets a browser client and
# an unauthenticated API. The entrypoint warns and serves anyway rather than
# withholding the surface. Note the scope — Paseo's password is DAEMON-wide, so
# setting it also makes the phone's direct connection ask for it. Written to
# ~/.devaloy_secrets (0600) like the tokens above.
# WITH_PASEO_WEB_UI=false
# PASEO_PASSWORD=

# Paseo plugins to install on every boot, whitespace-separated. Two forms:
#
#   owner/repo:plugins/<name>   a plugin in a git repository
#   /home/dev/projects/x/y      a directory already on the box
#
# The runtime id defaults to the last path segment. Prefix an entry with `id=`
# when the plugin's manifest id differs from its directory name:
#
#   PASEO_PLUGINS=kit-launcher=mimukit/paseo-plugins:plugins/kit-launcher
#
# A plugin whose id is already installed is left alone, so a plugin you disabled
# in the app stays disabled and adding an entry installs only the new one. A
# private repo needs credentials on the box; a failing entry logs a warning and
# never stops the box from starting.
#
# This is separate from config/paseo/config.json on purpose. That file records a
# plugin only after `paseo plugin install` has fetched it, and a git-sourced
# plugin also needs its clone under ~/.paseo/plugins.
# PASEO_PLUGINS=mimukit/paseo-plugins:plugins/kit-launcher mimukit/paseo-plugins:plugins/worktree-sync

# Let an agent on the box render a page and screenshot it. Off by default: it
# takes the image from 660 MB to 1.09 GB, and the first boot then downloads a
# 982 MB Chromium into the home volume.
#
# This key is BOTH a build arg and a runtime variable, and it needs both halves.
# The build arg puts the shared libraries a headless Chromium needs into the
# image, so changing it needs `docker compose up -d --build`, like WITH_ORCA.
# The runtime half makes the boot bootstrap install `playwright-cli` and the
# Chromium build into the home volume, and exports three defaults into every
# shell: the bundled Chromium as the browser, Chromium's own sandbox kept ON,
# and /tmp/playwright-cli as the screenshot directory. Setting it back to false
# uninstalls nothing.
#
# The flow is three commands: `playwright-cli open <url>`, then
# `playwright-cli screenshot`, which prints a path under /tmp/playwright-cli,
# then `playwright-cli close`. Paseo runs in this same container and opens that
# path directly. /tmp is not a volume, so screenshots die with the container.
# WITH_BROWSER=false

# TS_HOSTNAME=devaloy
# TS_ACCEPT_DNS=false
# MISE_NODE_VERSION=24
# MISE_HERDR_VERSION=latest

# ── Resource ceilings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All OFF by default (0 / -1 = unlimited), because the right numbers depend on
# the host and devaloy should not assume one. Leave them alone on a machine you
# have to yourself. Set them when devaloy SHARES a box with anything you care
# about, which is the case that matters: without a mem_limit this container can
# grow until the whole host is out of memory, and the kernel then kills whatever
# scores worst, not whatever you would have picked.
#
# Sizing rule. Add up every container's mem_limit, add a reserve for the host
# itself (dockerd, sshd, the kernel; 512m is a sane floor), and keep the total
# under physical RAM. Satisfy that and a host-wide OOM stops being possible:
# every kill lands inside one container's cgroup and the VM stays up.
#
# Worked example, a 3.73 GiB / 2 vCPU VPS also running two WordPress sites
# behind Traefik (512m host reserve + 768m WordPress + 576m MariaDB + 96m
# Traefik + 1664m here = 3616m, leaving ~200m of float):
#
#   DEVALOY_MEM_LIMIT=1664m
#   DEVALOY_MEMSWAP_LIMIT=3328m
#   DEVALOY_MEM_RESERVATION=512m
#   DEVALOY_CPUS=1.5
#   DEVALOY_CPU_SHARES=512
#   DEVALOY_PIDS_LIMIT=1024
#
# MEMSWAP is RAM+swap COMBINED, not swap alone, so it has to be >= MEM_LIMIT and
# the gap is the swap allowance. The host needs real swap for it to mean
# anything. Idle agent sessions are cold pages that swap well, so a wide gap
# turns "your third session got killed" into "your third session took a moment
# to wake up".
#
# CPUS is in cores: 1.5 on a 2-vCPU box leaves half a core that agents can never
# take, which is what keeps sshd and the reverse proxy responsive while a build
# runs. CPU_SHARES below 1024 only applies when something else wants the CPU at
# the same time.
#
# Sizing them, and the host-side pieces that go with them (earlyoom, log
# rotation, swappiness), are in docs/wiki/vm-resource-limits.md.
# DEVALOY_MEM_LIMIT=
# DEVALOY_MEMSWAP_LIMIT=
# DEVALOY_MEM_RESERVATION=
# DEVALOY_CPUS=
# DEVALOY_CPU_SHARES=
# DEVALOY_PIDS_LIMIT=

# /dev/shm. Docker's default is 64 MB and a headless Chromium crashes at that on
# a heavy page. Playwright's own advice is `--ipc=host`, which would share the
# HOST's IPC namespace with this container; a bigger tmpfs fixes the same crash
# without it. Costs nothing until written to, and then counts against
# DEVALOY_MEM_LIMIT. Applies whether WITH_BROWSER is on or off.
# DEVALOY_SHM_SIZE=512m
